// crew#561 (Otto fully enabled), superseding the crew#516 CP5 / crew#562 shape of this file.
// The founder's locked spec is unchanged in intent — "access is structurally locked down by
// identity, allowing only the Kubernetes cluster tag (tag:k8s) to communicate with the Mac host" —
// and one mechanism changed because it was measured impossible on his machine:
//
//   - Tailscale SSH "does not run in sandboxed Tailscale GUI builds": `tailscale up --ssh` on the
//     founder's Mac answers "500 The Tailscale SSH server does not run in sandboxed Tailscale GUI
//     builds" (2026-08-29, this Mac). tailscale.com/kb/1193/tailscale-ssh: "Requires use of open
//     source tailscale + tailscaled CLI devices". Replacing the founder's App Store client with the
//     open-source daemon is a hand on his machine every reinstall; refused (LAW 31, R52).
//
// The Mac carries `tag:founder-mac` — measured, not assumed: `tailscale status --self --json` on
// the Mac on 2026-08-29 answers Tags ["tag:founder-mac"]. A tagged device has no user identity
// (tailscale.com/kb/1068/tags), so a rule written against the founder's login or `autogroup:self`
// would never match it and would lock both the cluster and his phone out; every rule that means
// "the Mac" therefore names the tag. Port 22 is macOS Remote Login (sshd), which was already
// listening, and the cluster's identity to sshd is an ed25519 key minted on a CI runner into the
// vault entry `hermes-mac-run` (bin/idp-bootstrap-macrun), mounted into the hermes-agent pod, with
// its public half adopted into the Mac's authorised-keys file by bin/idp-mac-adopt-otto. Nobody
// types a key; nobody pastes one.
//
// The ACL rules are what remains of "locked down by identity", and they are exact:
//   - `tag:k8s` (the operator's egress proxies and the hermes-agent sidecar; tagOwners below) may
//     reach the Mac on 22 (mac-run, sshd) and 5900 (Guacamole's VNC egress,
//     platform/guacamole/mac-egress.yaml) and nothing else.
//   - `group:founder` (his login, and only his) may reach the Mac on any port: the iOS Shortcut's
//     `caffeinate` over 22, Moonlight to Sunshine on 47984-48010, screen sharing. His untagged
//     devices also reach each other (`autogroup:self`, kb/1337: "devices where the same user is
//     authenticated on both the source and the destination"). Nobody else's device reaches his.
//   - A policy file with an `acls` section is deny-by-default (tailscale.com/kb/1018/acls:
//     "Using a default deny policy prevents communication between devices without explicit
//     access to each other"); every other pair is refused.
//
// `group:founder` names the login, never `autogroup:member`: membership is dynamic, and the first
// person added later — a buyer's engineer in diligence, a contractor — would otherwise silently
// get the founder's desktop (peer review, session 78caaa17 on idp#606). The tailnet-user placeholder below is
// the one placeholder this file carries (LAW 46); bin/idp-tailscale-policy substitutes it from
// clusters/oke/estate-config.yaml or, when that is empty, from the tailnet's owner record, and
// refuses to PUT a body in which any placeholder survived (the applier greps the rendered text, comments included — incident 2026-08-29, oke-check run 33280019151). Applied by CI (oke-check's apply job), never by
// hand in the admin console. No `ssh` section: with no Tailscale SSH server on the destination,
// an ssh rule is a promise nothing keeps; sshd decides port 22 (kb/1193, "permitting both" applies
// only where Tailscale SSH runs).
{
  "groups": {
    // One member today. A second one is a deliberate edit to this file with a review on it.
    "group:founder": ["${FOUNDER_TAILNET_USER}"],
  },
  "tagOwners": {
    // [] = admins only: no login can tag a new device into either role by itself.
    "tag:k8s":         [],
    // the k8s operator's own device (chart 1.102.3 default operatorConfig.defaultTags);
    // absent from here = authkey mint 400s and the operator crashloops (P0 idp#586, run 33374905345).
    // Owned by tag:k8s, not []: the root OAuth client (tagged tag:k8s) mints the operator client,
    // and a client may only request tags its own tags own (kb/1215) — [] left it no path and the
    // mint 400d again with the policy already applied (run 33447033447, 22:43Z vs apply 22:40Z).
    "tag:k8s-operator": ["tag:k8s"],
    "tag:founder-mac": [],
    // crew#841: the break-glass bridge instance, so debugging does not depend on the founder's
    // laptop being awake. Owned by tag:k8s for the same reason tag:k8s-operator is: the root
    // OAuth client carries tag:k8s, and a client may only request tags its own tags own
    // (kb/1215). [] here would 400 the join exactly as it did for the operator.
    "tag:estate-bridge": ["tag:k8s"],
  },
  "acls": [
    {"action": "accept", "src": ["tag:k8s"],
     "dst":    ["tag:founder-mac:22", "tag:founder-mac:5900"]},
    {"action": "accept", "src": ["group:founder"],
     "dst":    ["tag:founder-mac:*", "autogroup:self:*"]},
    // His phone reads the cluster through the bridge, and only the one port the bridge serves.
    {"action": "accept", "src": ["group:founder"],
     "dst":    ["tag:estate-bridge:8001"]},
  ],
}
